AWS Security Layers: Internet Gateway, Security Groups & NACLs

Internet Gateway (IGW) - Core Characteristics

1. IGW-VPC Association Rules:

Primary Rule:
✅ One IGW per VPC (maximum)
✅ Multiple IGWs can be created, but for different VPCs
❌ Multiple IGWs cannot be attached to a single VPC

Strategic Approach:
Create IGWs in advance for future VPC deployments to streamline provisioning.

2. IGW Technical Features:
Horizontally Scaled: AWS automatically handles scaling
Redundant & Highly Available: Never experiences downtime
Dual Protocol Support: Native IPv4 & IPv6 compatibility

AWS Managed: Backed by multiple ISP connections
No Bandwidth Constraints: Automatically scales with traffic
Security Groups (SG) - Instance-Level Firewall

What are Security Groups?

EC2 instance personal firewall
Virtual NIC-level protection
Stateful firewall: Allowed inbound traffic automatically permits return traffic
Last line of defense for instance security

Real-World Analogy:
EC2 Instance = Your House
Security Group = House Security Guard
Guard checks every visitor before entry

Key Characteristics:

Operates at instance level
Supports only ALLOW rules (implicit deny everything else)
All rules evaluated (no priority order)
Can reference other security groups
Associated with network interfaces

Network ACLs (NACL) - Subnet-Level Firewall

What are Network ACLs?
Subnet perimeter security

Stateless firewall: Requires explicit rules for return traffic
First line of defense - Filters traffic before entering subnet
Optional security layer


Real-World Analogy:
Subnet = Housing Society
NACL = Society Main Gate Security
Security Group = Individual House Security Guard

Key Characteristics:

Operates at subnet level
Supports ALLOW & DENY rules
Rule number priority (evaluated sequentially)
Stateless filtering (bidirectional rules required)
Default NACL allows all traffic

Security Group vs NACL Comparison
Feature	              Security Group (SG)	                  Network ACL (NACL)
Operational           Level	Instance                        level	Subnet level
State  Management    	Stateful                            	Stateless
Rule Types	          Allow rules only                      Allow & Deny rules
Rule Evaluation       All rules evaluated	                  Sequential by rule number
Defense Position	    Last line of defense	                First line of defense
Default Behavior	    Deny all inbound, Allow all outbound	Allow all inbound/outbound
Rule Reference	      Can reference other SGs	              IP ranges only
Association	          With ENI (instance)	                  With subnets

Complete Traffic Flow Analysis

Inbound Traffic Path:
Internet → IGW → Route Table → NACL (Subnet) → Security Group (EC2) → Instance

Step-by-Step Flow:
Step 1: Internet Gateway (IGW)
Entry point to VPC from internet
No traffic filtering performed

Step 2: Route Table
Determines traffic destination based on CIDR
Routes to appropriate subnet

Step 3: Network ACL (NACL)
First security checkpoint
Subnet-level traffic filtering
Stateless rule evaluation
Can explicitly deny malicious IP ranges

Step 4: Security Group (SG)
Final security checkpoint
Instance-specific filtering
Stateful connection tracking
Application-level security

Outbound Traffic Path (Reverse):
Instance → Security Group → NACL (Subnet) → Route Table → IGW → Internet
Practical Implementation Strategy

When to Use NACLs:
Subnet-level blocking (entire IP ranges)
DDoS mitigation at perimeter
Compliance requirements (explicit deny rules)
Multi-tenant environments (strict isolation)

When Security Groups Suffice:
Standard web applications
Development environments
Microservices architectures
Most production workloads

