Best Practices & Recommendations

Security Groups (Primary Defense):

Principle of least privilege - Minimum necessary ports
Reference security groups instead of IP addresses
Regular auditing of security group rules
Use descriptive names for rule tracking

Network ACLs (Supplementary Defense):

Default deny all at end of rule list
Block known malicious IPs at perimeter
Use for compliance controls when required
Monitor with VPC Flow Logs

Layered Security Approach:
Internet Traffic → IGW → Route Table → NACL → Security Group → Instance
                  ↓          ↓           ↓          ↓           ↓
              No Filter   Routing    Subnet     Instance    Application
                                 Perimeter    Perimeter    Security

Architectural Insight: While Security Groups typically provide sufficient protection for most workloads, NACLs offer an additional security layer for specific use cases. The combination creates a defense-in-depth strategy where NACLs act as the perimeter fence and Security Groups serve as individual building security systems. Most organizations find Security Groups alone are adequate, reserving NACLs for compliance requirements or specific threat mitigation scenarios.
